WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.380 line:88%
Communication approaches differ in how they make language accessible.

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:07.660 line:88%
Auditory verbal therapy develops spoken language through listening, with

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:09.180 line:88%
active caregiver participation.

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:13.680 line:88%
Auditory oral teaching also uses speechreading and natural visual

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:14.380 line:88%
cues.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:17.560 line:88%
Watch the information available to the learner change when

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:19.720 line:88%
the visible speech cue joins the listening task.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:23.960 line:88%
The goal is spontaneous spoken communication in everyday life,

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:25.960 line:88%
supported by consistent amplification.

00:00:27.254 --> 00:00:30.934 line:88%
Cued Speech adds phonemic hand cues to visible speech.

00:00:31.154 --> 00:00:34.274 line:88%
The hand cue and mouth movement work together to

00:00:34.274 --> 00:00:35.994 line:88%
distinguish sounds that look alike.

00:00:36.794 --> 00:00:39.694 line:88%
This is different from American Sign Language, a language

00:00:39.694 --> 00:00:40.694 line:88%
with its own structure.

00:00:41.494 --> 00:00:44.894 line:88%
In the bilingual bicultural approach described in this lecture,

00:00:45.154 --> 00:00:48.094 line:88%
American Sign Language comes first and written English is

00:00:48.094 --> 00:00:49.714 line:88%
taught as a second language.

00:00:50.614 --> 00:00:54.614 line:88%
Total Communication combines several communication tools using an English

00:00:54.614 --> 00:00:56.114 line:88%
based sign system.

00:00:56.914 --> 00:01:00.404 line:88%
Choose an approach around the child, family participation, and

00:01:00.404 --> 00:01:01.364 line:88%
language access.
